1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,234
Sweetheart, every Christmas morning I watch my

2
00:00:03,234 --> 00:00:06,820
grandchildren rip through forty dollars of wrapping

3
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:08,788
paper in about four minutes.

4
00:00:08,968 --> 00:00:11,179
Shiny paper, plastic bows,

5
00:00:11,179 --> 00:00:14,155
tape everywhere, and it all goes in

6
00:00:14,155 --> 00:00:17,131
a black trash bag before the coffee

7
00:00:17,131 --> 00:00:17,896
is ready.

8
00:00:18,076 --> 00:00:19,797
And I think of my mother,

9
00:00:19,797 --> 00:00:22,343
who wrapped every present we ever got

10
00:00:22,343 --> 00:00:23,650
for almost nothing,

11
00:00:23,650 --> 00:00:25,783
and they were so pretty that we

12
00:00:25,783 --> 00:00:27,504
didn't want to open them.

13
00:00:27,854 --> 00:00:31,100
So tonight, the seven gift wrapping mistakes

14
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:33,682
that make your presents look cheap,

15
00:00:33,682 --> 00:00:35,969
and exactly what to do instead.

16
00:00:36,149 --> 00:00:38,912
Most of it costs nothing at all.

17
00:00:39,092 --> 00:00:41,189
Some of it you already have in

18
00:00:41,189 --> 00:00:42,587
your kitchen drawer.

19
00:00:42,767 --> 00:00:43,521
By the end,

20
00:00:43,521 --> 00:00:46,059
your presents will be the ones people

21
00:00:46,059 --> 00:00:48,734
take pictures of before they open them.

22
00:00:49,085 --> 00:00:50,039
In our house,

23
00:00:50,039 --> 00:00:52,904
Christmas wrapping was a whole evening.

24
00:00:53,084 --> 00:00:55,043
The Sunday before Christmas,

25
00:00:55,043 --> 00:00:57,910
my mother cleared the kitchen table after

26
00:00:57,910 --> 00:01:00,219
supper, spread out a clean sheet,

27
00:01:00,219 --> 00:01:02,737
and brought out the shoebox with the

28
00:01:02,737 --> 00:01:05,534
ribbon and the good scissors that nobody

29
00:01:05,534 --> 00:01:07,353
else was allowed to touch.

30
00:01:07,533 --> 00:01:09,346
Danny and I had to stay in

31
00:01:09,346 --> 00:01:10,393
the front room,

32
00:01:10,393 --> 00:01:12,903
but we could hear the paper crinkle,

33
00:01:12,903 --> 00:01:14,996
and we'd try to guess what was

34
00:01:14,996 --> 00:01:16,809
in every box by the sound.

35
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:18,820
Mistake number one.

36
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:20,985
Cutting the paper too big.

37
00:01:21,165 --> 00:01:23,425
You end up with thick wads of

38
00:01:23,425 --> 00:01:25,996
paper folded over and over at the

39
00:01:25,996 --> 00:01:27,554
ends, lumpy corners,

40
00:01:27,554 --> 00:01:29,891
and so much tape the box looks

41
00:01:29,891 --> 00:01:30,592
bandaged.

42
00:01:30,772 --> 00:01:33,724
A present with fat ends always looks

43
00:01:33,724 --> 00:01:37,004
cheap, no matter how expensive the paper

44
00:01:37,004 --> 00:01:37,332
was.

45
00:01:37,682 --> 00:01:39,249
Do this instead.

46
00:01:39,429 --> 00:01:40,358
Measure.

47
00:01:40,538 --> 00:01:42,798
The paper should go around the box

48
00:01:42,798 --> 00:01:44,926
with just two inches of overlap,

49
00:01:44,926 --> 00:01:46,921
and on the ends it should come

50
00:01:46,921 --> 00:01:49,315
up only three quarters of the height

51
00:01:49,315 --> 00:01:50,046
of the box.

52
00:01:50,226 --> 00:01:52,348
My mother laid the box on the

53
00:01:52,348 --> 00:01:54,981
paper, rolled it once around to see,

54
00:01:54,981 --> 00:01:57,395
and cut with her good scissors in

55
00:01:57,395 --> 00:01:58,493
one long slide.

56
00:01:58,673 --> 00:02:01,116
Then she folded the edge of the

57
00:02:01,116 --> 00:02:03,323
overlap under, half an inch,

58
00:02:03,323 --> 00:02:05,530
so there was no raw cut edge

59
00:02:05,530 --> 00:02:06,161
showing.

60
00:02:06,341 --> 00:02:07,676
Clean as a store.

61
00:02:08,027 --> 00:02:10,564
And for a box that isn't square,

62
00:02:10,564 --> 00:02:12,784
honey, a round tin or an odd

63
00:02:12,784 --> 00:02:14,528
shape, don't fight it.

64
00:02:14,708 --> 00:02:16,444
Set it in the middle of a

65
00:02:16,444 --> 00:02:17,833
big square of paper,

66
00:02:17,833 --> 00:02:20,194
gather the paper up around it like

67
00:02:20,194 --> 00:02:21,860
a bag, and tie it at the

68
00:02:21,860 --> 00:02:22,971
top with ribbon,

69
00:02:22,971 --> 00:02:25,055
so it looks like a little sack

70
00:02:25,055 --> 00:02:25,680
of candy.

71
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:28,811
My mother wrapped Grandma Pearl's round tins

72
00:02:28,811 --> 00:02:31,361
of homemade candy that way every year,

73
00:02:31,361 --> 00:02:34,044
and they were the prettiest things under

74
00:02:34,044 --> 00:02:34,648
the tree.

75
00:02:34,999 --> 00:02:36,810
Mistake number two.

76
00:02:36,990 --> 00:02:39,254
Tape everywhere you can see it.

77
00:02:39,434 --> 00:02:42,364
Long crooked strips across the top,

78
00:02:42,364 --> 00:02:44,122
tape on every corner,

79
00:02:44,122 --> 00:02:45,378
tape over tape.

80
00:02:45,558 --> 00:02:47,892
Tape is the first thing the eye

81
00:02:47,892 --> 00:02:49,021
goes to, honey,

82
00:02:49,021 --> 00:02:51,655
and it makes even a beautiful paper

83
00:02:51,655 --> 00:02:52,559
look rushed.

84
00:02:52,909 --> 00:02:54,244
Do this instead.

85
00:02:54,424 --> 00:02:56,095
Use double-sided tape,

86
00:02:56,095 --> 00:02:58,602
or roll a little piece of regular

87
00:02:58,602 --> 00:03:00,729
tape into a loop and hide it

88
00:03:00,729 --> 00:03:02,400
under the folded edge.

89
00:03:02,580 --> 00:03:05,773
Three small hidden pieces are enough for

90
00:03:05,773 --> 00:03:06,411
any box.

91
00:03:06,591 --> 00:03:08,488
In nineteen fifty-three,

92
00:03:08,488 --> 00:03:09,989
tape was expensive,

93
00:03:09,989 --> 00:03:12,281
so my mother used just one or

94
00:03:12,281 --> 00:03:14,811
two pieces and a ribbon held the

95
00:03:14,811 --> 00:03:15,206
rest.

96
00:03:15,386 --> 00:03:18,018
Nobody ever saw a single piece of

97
00:03:18,018 --> 00:03:19,693
tape on her presents.

98
00:03:20,043 --> 00:03:22,666
One more trick for crisp edges.

99
00:03:22,846 --> 00:03:24,830
After you fold each end,

100
00:03:24,830 --> 00:03:28,218
run your thumbnail hard along every fold,

101
00:03:28,218 --> 00:03:30,532
all twelve edges of the box.

102
00:03:30,712 --> 00:03:32,510
It makes a sharp line,

103
00:03:32,510 --> 00:03:34,799
like a pressed shirt collar.

104
00:03:34,979 --> 00:03:37,231
My mother called it ironing the present.

105
00:03:37,411 --> 00:03:38,819
It takes one minute,

106
00:03:38,819 --> 00:03:42,057
and it's the whole difference between homemade

107
00:03:42,057 --> 00:03:42,972
and handsome.

108
00:03:43,323 --> 00:03:45,273
Mistake number three.

109
00:03:45,453 --> 00:03:47,404
The plastic stick-on bow.

110
00:03:47,584 --> 00:03:49,695
It's the one thing that says,

111
00:03:49,695 --> 00:03:51,589
I wrapped this in the car.

112
00:03:51,769 --> 00:03:53,730
They get crushed in the bag,

113
00:03:53,730 --> 00:03:54,711
they fall off,

114
00:03:54,711 --> 00:03:57,022
and they're all the same in every

115
00:03:57,022 --> 00:03:58,213
house in America.

116
00:03:58,563 --> 00:04:00,281
Do this instead.

117
00:04:00,461 --> 00:04:03,166
Real ribbon, tied by hand.

118
00:04:03,346 --> 00:04:05,413
Cloth ribbon if you can,

119
00:04:05,413 --> 00:04:06,962
or plain red yarn,

120
00:04:06,962 --> 00:04:08,512
or kitchen string.

121
00:04:08,692 --> 00:04:10,926
Go around the box both ways,

122
00:04:10,926 --> 00:04:13,958
crossed underneath, and tie the bow on

123
00:04:13,958 --> 00:04:14,277
top.

124
00:04:14,457 --> 00:04:16,918
And here's Grandma Pearl's trick.

125
00:04:17,098 --> 00:04:18,967
Tuck something into the knot.

126
00:04:19,147 --> 00:04:20,842
A sprig of pine.

127
00:04:21,022 --> 00:04:22,659
A cinnamon stick.

128
00:04:22,839 --> 00:04:24,291
A candy cane.

129
00:04:24,471 --> 00:04:25,655
A little bell.

130
00:04:25,835 --> 00:04:27,243
It costs pennies,

131
00:04:27,243 --> 00:04:29,893
and it looks like a present from

132
00:04:29,893 --> 00:04:30,804
a magazine.

133
00:04:31,154 --> 00:04:32,883
And save your ribbon,

134
00:04:32,883 --> 00:04:33,789
sweetheart.

135
00:04:33,969 --> 00:04:35,842
When the presents are opened,

136
00:04:35,842 --> 00:04:38,167
roll the good ribbons around a piece

137
00:04:38,167 --> 00:04:40,234
of cardboard and put them in the

138
00:04:40,234 --> 00:04:41,655
shoebox for next year.

139
00:04:41,835 --> 00:04:44,424
My mother had red velvet ribbon that

140
00:04:44,424 --> 00:04:47,805
went around our presents for eleven Christmases

141
00:04:47,805 --> 00:04:48,453
in a row.

142
00:04:48,633 --> 00:04:50,927
When it finally got too short,

143
00:04:50,927 --> 00:04:52,915
she cut it up and tied the

144
00:04:52,915 --> 00:04:54,368
pieces on the tree.

145
00:04:54,718 --> 00:04:56,564
Mistake number four.

146
00:04:56,744 --> 00:04:59,739
Every present in a different loud paper.

147
00:04:59,919 --> 00:05:02,131
Santas, snowmen, stripes,

148
00:05:02,131 --> 00:05:05,051
glitter, all under the same tree.

149
00:05:05,231 --> 00:05:07,065
It looks like a garage sale,

150
00:05:07,065 --> 00:05:09,489
honey, and none of the presents stand

151
00:05:09,489 --> 00:05:11,651
out because they're all shouting.

152
00:05:12,001 --> 00:05:13,603
Do this instead.

153
00:05:13,783 --> 00:05:16,191
Pick one plain paper for everything,

154
00:05:16,191 --> 00:05:17,997
and change only the ribbon.

155
00:05:18,177 --> 00:05:20,910
Brown kraft paper is the cheapest paper

156
00:05:20,910 --> 00:05:23,151
there is, and with red ribbon it

157
00:05:23,151 --> 00:05:24,272
looks beautiful.

158
00:05:24,452 --> 00:05:26,949
White butcher paper with green ribbon.

159
00:05:27,129 --> 00:05:29,784
My mother used plain brown paper from

160
00:05:29,784 --> 00:05:30,645
the A and P,

161
00:05:30,645 --> 00:05:32,798
the kind they wrapped meat in,

162
00:05:32,798 --> 00:05:34,663
and tied it with red yarn.

163
00:05:34,843 --> 00:05:36,472
The whole tree matched,

164
00:05:36,472 --> 00:05:38,384
and people stopped to look.

165
00:05:38,735 --> 00:05:40,560
If you want to dress up the

166
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:41,777
plain brown paper,

167
00:05:41,777 --> 00:05:43,332
let the children do it.

168
00:05:43,512 --> 00:05:45,186
A potato cut in half,

169
00:05:45,186 --> 00:05:46,701
carved into a star,

170
00:05:46,701 --> 00:05:49,491
dipped in red paint and stamped all

171
00:05:49,491 --> 00:05:50,687
over the paper.

172
00:05:50,867 --> 00:05:53,925
Or little white fingerprints that look like

173
00:05:53,925 --> 00:05:54,280
snow.

174
00:05:54,460 --> 00:05:57,320
Danny and I stamped the paper every

175
00:05:57,320 --> 00:05:59,853
year, crooked stars everywhere,

176
00:05:59,853 --> 00:06:02,876
and Grandma Pearl kept every piece we

177
00:06:02,876 --> 00:06:05,490
ever stamped in her cedar chest.

178
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:07,686
Mistake number five.

179
00:06:07,866 --> 00:06:10,256
Buying a new gift bag for every

180
00:06:10,256 --> 00:06:10,873
present.

181
00:06:11,053 --> 00:06:13,678
They cost three or four dollars each,

182
00:06:13,678 --> 00:06:16,232
sometimes more than the gift inside,

183
00:06:16,232 --> 00:06:18,786
and the tissue paper sticks out like

184
00:06:18,786 --> 00:06:19,992
a feather duster.

185
00:06:20,343 --> 00:06:21,910
Do this instead.

186
00:06:22,090 --> 00:06:23,925
Wrap in things that are part of

187
00:06:23,925 --> 00:06:24,458
the gift.

188
00:06:24,638 --> 00:06:27,339
A kitchen towel tied around a jar

189
00:06:27,339 --> 00:06:27,912
of jam.

190
00:06:28,092 --> 00:06:30,472
A pretty scarf around a book.

191
00:06:30,652 --> 00:06:33,323
A cookie tin from the thrift store.

192
00:06:33,503 --> 00:06:35,715
A Mason jar with a square of

193
00:06:35,715 --> 00:06:37,531
red cloth over the lid.

194
00:06:37,711 --> 00:06:40,579
The Japanese have wrapped gifts in cloth

195
00:06:40,579 --> 00:06:42,157
for hundreds of years,

196
00:06:42,157 --> 00:06:44,379
and my mother did it with flour

197
00:06:44,379 --> 00:06:46,387
sack cloth in the war years,

198
00:06:46,387 --> 00:06:48,323
because paper was rationed.

199
00:06:48,673 --> 00:06:50,902
And here's a trick for gifts that

200
00:06:50,902 --> 00:06:52,051
are hard to hide.

201
00:06:52,231 --> 00:06:53,921
A bicycle, a sled,

202
00:06:53,921 --> 00:06:56,738
a big box that won't fit under

203
00:06:56,738 --> 00:06:57,583
the tree.

204
00:06:57,763 --> 00:06:59,273
Don't wrap it at all.

205
00:06:59,453 --> 00:07:01,219
Hide it in the garage or at

206
00:07:01,219 --> 00:07:03,444
the neighbor's, and tie one end of

207
00:07:03,444 --> 00:07:05,014
a long red string to it.

208
00:07:05,194 --> 00:07:07,441
Run the string through the house and

209
00:07:07,441 --> 00:07:09,438
tie the other end to the child's

210
00:07:09,438 --> 00:07:10,999
bedpost on Christmas Eve.

211
00:07:11,179 --> 00:07:13,420
In the morning they follow the string.

212
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:16,316
Danny followed one to his first bicycle

213
00:07:16,316 --> 00:07:17,918
in nineteen fifty-four,

214
00:07:17,918 --> 00:07:20,148
and he talked about it until the

215
00:07:20,148 --> 00:07:20,983
day he died.

216
00:07:21,334 --> 00:07:23,261
Mistake number six.

217
00:07:23,441 --> 00:07:26,323
A store-bought sticker that says To and

218
00:07:26,323 --> 00:07:27,432
From, or worse,

219
00:07:27,432 --> 00:07:28,540
nothing at all,

220
00:07:28,540 --> 00:07:31,349
and then nobody knows whose present is

221
00:07:31,349 --> 00:07:33,344
whose on Christmas morning.

222
00:07:33,694 --> 00:07:35,122
Do this instead.

223
00:07:35,302 --> 00:07:38,947
Make tags from last year's Christmas cards.

224
00:07:39,127 --> 00:07:40,908
Cut out the pretty picture,

225
00:07:40,908 --> 00:07:41,765
punch a hole,

226
00:07:41,765 --> 00:07:43,546
write the name on the back,

227
00:07:43,546 --> 00:07:45,524
and tie it on with the ribbon.

228
00:07:45,704 --> 00:07:48,750
My mother saved every Christmas card in

229
00:07:48,750 --> 00:07:50,859
a shoebox for exactly that.

230
00:07:51,039 --> 00:07:53,902
And write something on the tag besides

231
00:07:53,902 --> 00:07:54,580
the name.

232
00:07:54,760 --> 00:07:56,920
A line, a memory.

233
00:07:57,100 --> 00:07:59,501
My father wrote a little joke on

234
00:07:59,501 --> 00:08:00,851
every one of mine,

235
00:08:00,851 --> 00:08:03,102
and I still have some of them.

236
00:08:03,452 --> 00:08:05,160
And for the little ones,

237
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:07,295
a tag with a riddle instead of

238
00:08:07,295 --> 00:08:07,794
a name.

239
00:08:07,974 --> 00:08:09,801
Who is the only one in this

240
00:08:09,801 --> 00:08:11,898
house who sings in the bathtub?

241
00:08:12,078 --> 00:08:14,286
Who left their boots in the hall

242
00:08:14,286 --> 00:08:15,805
three times this week?

243
00:08:15,985 --> 00:08:18,028
My mother did it one year and

244
00:08:18,028 --> 00:08:20,283
we laughed so hard my father had

245
00:08:20,283 --> 00:08:22,115
to stop the whole morning.

246
00:08:22,295 --> 00:08:25,766
Now my grandchildren beg for riddle tags.

247
00:08:26,116 --> 00:08:27,822
Mistake number seven.

248
00:08:28,002 --> 00:08:31,628
Wrapping everything on Christmas Eve at midnight,

249
00:08:31,628 --> 00:08:34,143
with the bad scissors and the last

250
00:08:34,143 --> 00:08:35,031
bit of tape,

251
00:08:35,031 --> 00:08:37,325
while everybody else is asleep.

252
00:08:37,505 --> 00:08:40,013
That's how presents end up looking cheap.

253
00:08:40,193 --> 00:08:41,644
Not from the paper.

254
00:08:41,824 --> 00:08:43,101
From being tired.

255
00:08:43,451 --> 00:08:45,053
Do this instead.

256
00:08:45,233 --> 00:08:46,730
Wrap as you buy.

257
00:08:46,910 --> 00:08:49,445
Keep a wrapping box under the bed

258
00:08:49,445 --> 00:08:50,674
with your paper,

259
00:08:50,674 --> 00:08:52,670
ribbon, scissors and tags,

260
00:08:52,670 --> 00:08:55,435
and every time a present comes home,

261
00:08:55,435 --> 00:08:57,278
wrap it that same night.

262
00:08:57,458 --> 00:08:59,908
One present takes five minutes.

263
00:09:00,088 --> 00:09:02,921
By the twentieth everything is done,

264
00:09:02,921 --> 00:09:05,596
and Christmas Eve is for cocoa and

265
00:09:05,596 --> 00:09:07,878
carols, the way it should be.

266
00:09:08,228 --> 00:09:10,415
And keep the mess under control on

267
00:09:10,415 --> 00:09:12,024
Christmas morning, honey.

268
00:09:12,204 --> 00:09:14,533
Put one big paper grocery sack by

269
00:09:14,533 --> 00:09:16,368
the tree for the wrapping,

270
00:09:16,368 --> 00:09:18,767
and one little box for the ribbons

271
00:09:18,767 --> 00:09:20,319
and tags worth saving.

272
00:09:20,499 --> 00:09:22,808
My father was in charge of the

273
00:09:22,808 --> 00:09:23,193
sack.

274
00:09:23,373 --> 00:09:26,024
Every time somebody opened a present,

275
00:09:26,024 --> 00:09:27,242
he'd hold it out,

276
00:09:27,242 --> 00:09:29,535
and we'd throw the paper in like

277
00:09:29,535 --> 00:09:30,467
a basketball.

278
00:09:30,647 --> 00:09:32,620
Two points for every shot.

279
00:09:32,971 --> 00:09:35,000
So here's the whole lesson,

280
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:35,827
sweetheart.

281
00:09:36,007 --> 00:09:37,504
Measure the paper.

282
00:09:37,684 --> 00:09:39,054
Hide the tape.

283
00:09:39,234 --> 00:09:42,101
Real ribbon, with something tucked in the

284
00:09:42,101 --> 00:09:42,450
knot.

285
00:09:42,630 --> 00:09:44,976
One paper for the whole tree.

286
00:09:45,156 --> 00:09:47,000
Wrap in things that are part of

287
